ed, is owner-held, named in the ruling, and not performed here. FIRST-RUN-ADOPTER — finding (F4, routed): the amended need is satisfiable from shipped guidance alone and no supported caller breaks, but an adopter reading `hats.toml`/`hats.py` is still told the record half is unbuilt. MAINTAINER — finding A1: two years out the reversal is recoverable from the ruling and LLR-183's discharge sentence; the one sentence that would mislead is the stale redesign-record paragraph. TEST-ENGINEER — finding A2: what detects a violation is TC-211's Inspection, demonstrated by the missing-outcome and paraphrasing-child cases; the stated residual is that SR-161 is Approved with Inspection while its only Inspection instrument is Drafted and incomplete — openly recorded, so not a separate finding. UX-DESIGNER — no finding: no surface changes layout or reading priority; the only rendered effect is SR-161's method label. UX-ENGINEER — no finding: no view is added or re-rendered; empty/malformed behaviour (absent roster = opt-out, unparseable = refusal) is untouched. ACCESSIBILITY — no finding: results are words (PASS / Historical INCOMPLETE), no colour-only signal, all evidence is text. PERFORMANCE — answered \\\\\\\"no bound needed\\\\\\\" in one line, as the charter permits: the change removes a per-need record and a checker, so review and operator cost falls; no speed, size or provider-usage risk. CONSISTENCY — finding A1 (and F4): template/instance divergence is unaffected; structure is unchanged. INTEGRITY-RECOVERABILITY — no finding: a half-applied write leaves SN-036 amended with SR-161 stale, but the state is nameable and the snapshot-drift arm surfaces amended-but-unreseeded rows. PRODUCT-FITNESS — no finding: the owner asked for a recorded review pass rather than a guard, and SN-036, SR-161, LLR-183, the procedure and TC-211 now deliver exactly that, with the refused shape absent.\\n\\nNON-APPLICABLE ROSTER HATS, WITH PREDICATE REASON (5). UNATTENDED-OPS — predicate tags contains \\\\\\\"unattended\\\\\\\" or \\\\\\\"loop\\\\\\\"; neither is declared, and no unattended path changes. CROSS-PLATFORM — predicate needs scripts/launcher/shell; none declared, and no script changes. SAFETY — predicate needs \\\\\\\"safety\\\\\\\"; undeclared, and no physical-harm referent exists in a process amendment. LEGAL — predicate needs \\\\\\\"legal\\\\\\\"; declared on SN-026, not on these parents. DATA-PROTECTION — predicate needs \\\\\\\"personal-data\\\\\\\"; undeclared, no personal data touched. Per hats.py, an undeclared field satisfies no clause, so each silence is by rule.\\n\\nEACH CHILD'S INDEPENDENT PURPOSE. SR-161 (SN-036) — the perspective obligation: applicable questions reach the brief, the sitting's record names each outcome. SR-184 (SN-024) — Critique acceptance provenance: fresh non-author session, intent-derived rubric, numbered anchors. SR-185 (SN-037) — the semantic counterpart decision SR-162's rationale expressly disclaims. SR-186 (SN-012) — proportionality and the stopping decision, owned by no other row. LLR-183 (SR-161) — a distinct mechanism, not a paraphrase: Hat-Refs as a resolvable cell, its severity split, the derived effective set; Inspection makes an LLR exempt, not forbidden. TC-178 — supplementary mechanical evidence for those mechanics. TC-209/210/211 — one direct Inspection each with distinct discriminating cases: missing provenance and TC-copied rubric; reference-only counterpart; missing perspective outcome and paraphrasing child.\\n\\nSTOPPING RATIONALE. No LLR is minted under SR-184/185/186: PROCESS.md exempts Inspection SRs, and such an LLR would restate a review judgment without defining a mechanism. SR-161 is not split into brief-side and record-side rows because one acceptance condition spans both and two rows would share one decision. TC-211 is not split per hat because the roster supplies applicability and a per-hat case would multiply cases without adding a judgment. Further splitting anywhere in this chain relabels the same question — the independent-value boundary SR-186 names.\\n\\nVERDICT AND ANCHORS. APPROVE. F1 (G4/B4), F2 (G3/B3) and F3 (G5/B5) are re-driven and closed on the revised source; the ruling's four acts are executed with no scope expansion and no fabricated owner reading. A1 cites G3/B3 and A2 cites G2/B2, both advisory and routed; A3 records state under G1/G3. No single advisory here reaches the bar for refusing closure.\"}","stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"aadfcce4-ccef-46e1-b154-790f5b27c6db","total_cost_usd":1.4595615000000002,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":93289,"cache_read_input_tokens":835,"output_tokens":18608,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":93289,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":18608,"cache_read_input_tokens":835,"cache_creation_input_tokens":93289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":93289},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":60949,"outputTokens":19,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.061044,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":18608,"cacheReadInputTokens":835,"cacheCreationInputTokens":93289,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.3985175,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"verdict":"APPROVE","findings":["A1 [G3/B3 — advisory, routed to the queued record-comment refresh; not blocking]. `docs/ai-template-redesign-2026-09-05-codex/DECOMPOSITION-AMENDMENTS.md` was edited in this sitting (its H5 paragraph now cites the SN-036 owner ruling and the hat-by-hat outcomes), but its closing \"Implementation review subject (2026-09-06)\" paragraph still says, in the present ten